WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.874
Welcome to Module Two: Data Preparation.

2
00:00:03.204 --> 00:00:06.887
Open models differ a little in architecture, but a lot in data.

3
00:00:07.687 --> 00:00:11.541
Picture a small team that scrapes web text and trains their first model.

4
00:00:11.871 --> 00:00:14.851
The early results look exciting, then the problems show.

5
00:00:15.651 --> 00:00:20.102
It recites cookie banners, repeats spam, and once prints a customer's email.

6
00:00:20.432 --> 00:00:23.795
And it aces a benchmark whose answers were in the training data.

7
00:00:24.595 --> 00:00:28.727
Fine-tuned as a chat assistant, it answers well, then never stops talking.

8
00:00:29.057 --> 00:00:31.844
Every one of these is a data bug, not a model bug.

9
00:00:32.644 --> 00:00:37.522
It starts with the tokenizer, because the model only ever sees integer ids.

10
00:00:37.852 --> 00:00:42.218
It sets sequence length, embedding size, and how well code and languages work.

11
00:00:43.018 --> 00:00:47.277
Lesson one traces BPE, merging the most frequent pair each time.

12
00:00:47.607 --> 00:00:53.296
In the lab, you'll train an 8,000-token vocabulary and compare it with GPT-2's.

13
00:00:54.096 --> 00:00:56.585
You'll add new tokens without breaking the model.

14
00:00:56.915 --> 00:01:00.555
And you'll see how the end-of-sequence token decides when a model stops.

15
00:01:01.355 --> 00:01:04.292
Lesson two turns raw web crawl into training tokens.

16
00:01:04.622 --> 00:01:09.543
Extract, identify language, filter, deduplicate, redact, and decontaminate.

17
00:01:10.343 --> 00:01:14.773
Heuristic filters catch boilerplate and junk, while classifiers score quality.

18
00:01:15.103 --> 00:01:19.895
Tune them carefully: aggressive filters can erase dialects and minority voices.

19
00:01:20.695 --> 00:01:25.019
Exact duplicates are easy to hash, but near-duplicates need MinHash.

20
00:01:25.349 --> 00:01:28.584
You'll tune its bands and rows to set the similarity threshold.

21
00:01:29.384 --> 00:01:33.750
Regular expressions redact emails, phone numbers and other identifiers.

22
00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:38.894
And 13-gram overlap checks remove benchmark questions, keeping scores honest.

23
00:01:39.694 --> 00:01:44.209
At scale, NVIDIA NeMo Curator runs the same steps on GPUs.

24
00:01:44.539 --> 00:01:47.412
You'll learn the ideas on a laptop, then see them scale.

25
00:01:48.212 --> 00:01:51.533
Lesson three turns conversations into fine-tuning data.

26
00:01:51.863 --> 00:01:57.274
You'll unify Alpaca, ShareGPT and messages formats, then apply a chat template.

27
00:01:58.074 --> 00:02:01.843
You'll mask prompt tokens out of the loss, so the model learns to answer.

28
00:02:02.173 --> 00:02:05.600
And you'll fix the padding bug behind the chatbot that never stopped.

29
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.659
Packing short examples into one sequence saves the compute padding would waste.

30
00:02:10.989 --> 00:02:13.969
Done right, one conversation never leaks into the next.

31
00:02:14.769 --> 00:02:17.085
By the end, you'll have trained a tokenizer,

32
00:02:17.135 --> 00:02:19.775
built a cleaning pipeline, and prepared chat data.

33
00:02:20.105 --> 00:02:24.641
Let's begin with lesson one: tokenizers, BPE and special tokens.
